# Preserve repository-pinned upstream sources byte-for-byte; NInfer-owned files retain Git's
# default whitespace checks.
/third_party/spdlog/include/** -whitespace
/third_party/spdlog/src/** -whitespace

# The Serve TTFT benchmark freezes its corpus and verifies it by sha256, so those bytes have to
# survive a checkout unchanged. core.autocrlf=true rewrites them to CRLF on Windows, which changes
# the digest without changing the content: 57 text fixtures under bench/fixtures/ttft and
# examples/cli/messages were CRLF in the worktree and LF in the manifest, and the runner refused the
# corpus on this port for that reason alone. Pin the text ones; the 56 PNGs are binary by git's own
# detection and the rule below does not name them.
bench/fixtures/ttft/**/*.json text eol=lf
bench/fixtures/ttft/**/*.txt text eol=lf
bench/fixtures/ttft/**/*.md text eol=lf
examples/cli/messages/**/*.json text eol=lf

# Windows batch and cmd files are CRLF by definition, and two separate gates depend on their exact
# bytes: check_profile_consistency compares each generated launcher against the table's render, and it
# parses the QUASAR_ARGS line out of the hand-written launcher_env.bat. Both passed on a Windows
# worktree and failed on CI, because nothing pinned the checkout: every one of these files was `i/lf`
# in the index and `w/crlf` here, so the bytes the gates read differed by line ending and nothing else.
#
# Pinning all of them, not just the two patterns that happened to fail first. The lesson from three
# separate line-ending faults today is that a byte-level comparison must not leave the checkout to
# decide, and naming files one at a time is how one gets missed.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
